Here's an example of how to automatically generate a table of contents in a LaTeX document:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{hyperref}  % Optional: for clickable links in TOC

\title{My Document Title}
\author{Author Name}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

% Generate the title
\maketitle

% Generate the table of contents
\tableofcontents
\newpage  % Optional: start content on a new page

\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction section.

\subsection{Background Information}
Some background information here.

\subsection{Objectives}
The main objectives of this document.

\section{Methodology}
Description of the methods used.

\subsection{Data Collection}
How data was collected.

\subsubsection{Survey Design}
Details about survey design.

\subsection{Analysis Techniques}
Statistical methods applied.

\section{Results}
Presentation of findings.

\section{Conclusion}
Summary and conclusions.

\end{document}
```

**Key commands for automatic TOC generation:**

1. **`\tableofcontents`** - This is the main command that generates the table of contents
2. **`\section{}`, `\subsection{}`, `\subsubsection{}`** - These sectioning commands automatically populate the TOC
3. **`\newpage`** - Optional command to start the actual content on a new page after the TOC

**How it works:**
- LaTeX automatically collects all sectioning commands and their page numbers
- The TOC is generated based on the document structure
- You need to compile the document **twice** for the TOC to appear correctly (first pass to collect page numbers, second pass to display them)

**Additional customization options:**
```latex
% Control depth of TOC entries
\setcounter{tocdepth}{3}  % Show up to subsubsections

% Control which sections appear in TOC
\setcounter{secnumdepth}{2}  % Number up to subsections
```

The `hyperref` package (included in the example) makes the TOC entries clickable links that jump to the corresponding sections in the document.

